NAME
       setenforce - switch SELinux between enforcing and permissive

SYNOPSIS
       setenforce Enforcing|Permissive|1|0

DESCRIPTION
       setenforce changes the SELinux mode of the running system. In
       enforcing mode (1) the policy blocks what it does not allow and logs
       it. In permissive mode (0) nothing is blocked, but the denials are
       still logged, which helps find out whether SELinux is the cause of a
       problem.

       The change lasts until the next reboot. To change the mode at boot,
       set SELINUX=enforcing or SELINUX=permissive in /etc/selinux/config
       (or add enforcing=0 to the kernel command line for one boot).

       setenforce cannot enable or disable SELinux. When SELinux is
       disabled, it fails; enabling it again needs the config file and a
       reboot (with a full relabel). Only root can run it.

EXAMPLES
       Find out whether SELinux blocks a service, then switch back:

           setenforce 0
           getenforce
           systemctl restart httpd
           setenforce 1

       Make enforcing mode stay after reboots:

           vi /etc/selinux/config      (SELINUX=enforcing)
           sestatus

SEE ALSO
       getenforce(8), sestatus(8), selinux(8)
